              {
                "text": "The rule: crop the smallest bounding box of the non-background (colored) object. Output is the bounding box of colored cells.\n\nWe need to produce demonstration inputs. Let me create various grids with a colored image on black background, and the crop is the bounding box.\n\nLet me pick simple examples with different shapes/positions/colors.\n\nExample 1: a single colored cell somewhere.\n\nExample 2: a colored rectangle.\n\nExample 3: a diagonal line / L shape.\n\nI should make sure the object is a single connected component of one color (verifier: objects(I,T,T,F) - univalued, diagonal connectivity, without_bg False means bg=None... actually without_bg F means bg is None, so all cells including background are objects. Hmm.\n\nWait: objects(I, T, T, F): univalued=True, diagonal=True, without_bg=False. So bg = None. Then all cells are considered, including 0s. univalued=True means object grows only within same value. So connected components of equal-valued cells with diagonal adjacency.\n\nThen x0 = frontiers(I): rows that are constant, columns that are constant. merged gives cells with color... then color(x1) is the color of first element of merged frontier set. hmm, x2 = color of that. Then x3 = palette(I) = set of all colors. x4 = other(x3, x2) = the color that isn't the frontier color.\n\nTypically frontiers would be the constant rows/cols. For a grid with black background, rows near top/bottom and columns near left/right might be all-black (constant rows), so x1 color = 0 (black). Then x4 is the non-black color. Then objects extracted with that color, subgrid.\n\nBut careful: if there are no constant rows/columns, frontiers returns empty set, merge gives empty, color of empty fails. So we need grids where some row or column is constant. E.g., all black rows at edges - yes if the object doesn't touch an entire edge row... Actually a constant row is any row where all cells in that row are equal. A black border row is constant. So need at